course: biology
lesson: 274
title: Terror birds, moas, giant insects and other lost giants
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: After the dinosaurs, the world grew new giants: terror birds taller than a man with beaks like axes, moas taller than a basketball hoop, and a hornless rhino as heavy as several elephants.
explain: Long before that, 300 million years ago, dragonfly cousins had wings as wide as a crow's and a millipede grew as long as a car, in steamy forests whose wood later turned into coal.
explain: Warmth and air helped: big insects breathe through tubes, which works better with more oxygen, and cold-blooded snakes like 13-metre Titanoboa can grow bigger where it is hotter.
explain: Islands play tricks with size: on Sicily and Crete elephants shrank to the size of a pony, while on other islands small animals grew big, like the Komodo dragon.
explain: In New Zealand the moas and the giant Haast's eagle that hunted them were gone within about 200 years after people arrived, a warning of how fast giants can vanish.
fact: Terror birds were meat-eating birds of South America with small wings they could not fly with, some about 3 metres tall, with hooked beaks like axes; they lived until about 2 million years ago.
fact: Moas were giant plant-eating birds of New Zealand, up to about 3.6 metres tall with the neck up; the Haast's eagle, the biggest eagle ever, hunted them.
fact: People arrived in New Zealand around 1300 and hunted the moas, which had never met a hunter on land before; the moas died out within about 200 years, and the Haast's eagle, having lost the big birds it hunted, died out with them.
fact: The elephant bird of Madagascar laid eggs bigger than about 150 hen's eggs together and was one of the heaviest birds ever; it died out about 1,000 years ago.
fact: Meganeura, a dragonfly cousin from 300 million years ago, had wings about 70 centimetres across; more oxygen in the air then helped big insects breathe through their tubes.
fact: Arthropleura was a millipede relative as long as a car that lived in the coal forests and probably ate plants; the coal we burn comes from those forests.
fact: Titanoboa was a snake about 13 metres long that lived in a hot swamp in Colombia 60 million years ago and probably ate fish and crocodiles.
fact: Gigantopithecus was a giant ape, maybe 3 metres tall, that lived in China until about 300,000 years ago; we know it only from teeth and jaws, and there is no evidence that Bigfoot exists.
fact: Sarcosuchus was a crocodile relative about 11 metres long that lived in Africa alongside dinosaurs; its snout ended in a bulb, like a gharial's.
fact: Paraceratherium, a hornless rhino of Asia, was about 5 metres tall at the shoulder, ate leaves from treetops and was the biggest land mammal ever.
fact: On islands such as Sicily and Crete elephants became pony-sized, because with little food and no big hunters, small bodies did better.
fact: Almost all big animals have been found, but many new kinds of small animals are named every year; one ocean project reported over 800 new kinds in 2024, and most living species are still unnamed.
exercise: What were terror birds? | Flightless meat-eating birds of South America | Giant flying birds of the mountains of Asia | Plant-eating birds of New Zealand's forests | Seabirds of Antarctica
exercise: What kind of beak did a terror bird have? | A big hooked beak like an axe | A long thin beak like a straw | A flat beak like a duck | No beak, only teeth
exercise: How tall could a moa be with its neck up? | About 3.6 metres | About 36 centimetres | About 36 metres | About 1 metre
exercise: Which bird hunted the moa? | The Haast's eagle | The terror bird | The elephant bird | The kiwi
exercise: When did the moas die out? | Within about 200 years after people arrived | About 66 million years ago, with the dinosaurs | Long before New Zealand existed | About 5 years ago
exercise: Where did the elephant bird live? | Madagascar | Australia | Iceland | New Guinea
exercise: How big was an elephant bird egg? | Bigger than about 150 hen's eggs together | About the same size as one hen's egg | The size of a pea | As big as a small family car
exercise: How wide were the wings of Meganeura? | About 70 centimetres, like a crow's | About 7 millimetres, like a gnat's | About 7 metres, like a small plane's | About 7 centimetres
exercise: Why could insects grow so big 300 million years ago? | The air had more oxygen to breathe through their tubes | There were no birds or anything else at all to eat them | The Sun was much colder, and insects love the cold | They ate dinosaurs
exercise: How long was Arthropleura? | As long as a car | As long as a pencil | As long as a train | As long as a fingernail
exercise: Where does the coal we burn come from? | Ancient forests like the ones Arthropleura lived in | Dinosaur bones that were squashed flat under the sea | Ash from old volcanoes | Dried-up seawater
exercise: How long was Titanoboa? | About 13 metres | About 1 metre | About 100 metres | About 30 centimetres
exercise: Why could Titanoboa grow so big? | Cold-blooded animals can grow bigger where it is hotter | It ate dinosaurs every day, and they made it grow huge | It lived in the snow, where snakes grow largest | It had two hearts
exercise: How do we know Gigantopithecus? | Only from teeth and jaws | From a whole frozen body | From photos | From footprints in snow
exercise: What was Sarcosuchus? | A crocodile relative about 11 metres long | A giant snake from the hot swamps of Colombia | A flying reptile | A giant fish
exercise: What was the biggest land mammal ever? | Paraceratherium, a hornless rhino | The woolly mammoth of the Ice Age | The African elephant | The giant ground sloth
exercise: What happened to elephants on islands like Sicily and Crete? | They became pony-sized | They grew to twice the normal size | They learned to swim away | They turned into hippos
exercise: Are new kinds of animals still being found? | Yes, many new small kinds every year | No, all animals have been named and listed | Only one new kind in a whole century | Only giant ones
test: «Bigfoot's real, it's that giant ape from China» What is the honest answer? | Gigantopithecus was real but died out long ago, and there is no evidence for Bigfoot | Yes, Gigantopithecus never died out and still lives in the forests of America today | Yes, Bigfoot was filmed clearly | No, giant apes never existed
test: Count them: a museum has 2 terror bird and 3 moa skeletons, and moas had no wings at all. How many legs and wings? | 10 legs and 4 wings | 10 legs and 10 wings | 20 legs and 4 wings | 5 legs and 4 wings
test: What would happen to a big-bodied elephant stuck on a small island with little food and no hunters? | Over many generations its kind would tend to get smaller | It would grow wings and fly home | Its kind would get bigger and bigger | It would turn into a Komodo dragon
test: «giant dragonflies, so the bugs today could get massive too if we wait?» What is the best answer? | Not in today's air: big insects need more oxygen than there is now | Yes, every bug in your garden will soon be the size of a crow or bigger | No, giant dragonflies were only ever in a cartoon | Yes, if you feed them more sugar
test: In a cartoon a giant snake lives in an icy mountain cave. Why is that unlike Titanoboa? | Titanoboa needed a hot swamp, since cold-blooded animals grow bigger where it is warm | Snakes cannot live in caves | Titanoboa lived far out in the open sea and never once came near any land at all | Titanoboa was warm-blooded like a bear
test: «why'd the moas go so fast, they were huge» What is the most likely reason? | People arrived and hunted them, and the moas had never met a land hunter like that | A huge volcano erupted and buried the whole of New Zealand under ash and lava | The Haast's eagle ate every one | They flew away to Australia
test: When the moas disappeared, why did the Haast's eagle die out too? | It lost the big animal it hunted | It caught a cold | People tamed it | It turned into a kiwi
test: Count them: one elephant bird egg is like 150 hen's eggs. How many hen's eggs are 2 elephant bird eggs like? | 300 | 152 | 150 | 75
test: Why does every lump of coal remind us of Arthropleura? | Coal comes from the ancient forests where it lived | Coal is made of squashed millipedes like Arthropleura | Arthropleura ate coal | Coal is its fossil dung
test: «there's no giants left to find, scientists found everything» What is true? | Almost all big animals are known, but most living species are still unnamed | Yes, every species is named | No, new giants are found every week | Scientists stopped looking in 1900
test: A giraffe and Paraceratherium both ate from treetops. Which statement is true? | Paraceratherium was a hornless rhino as heavy as several elephants | Paraceratherium was a kind of giraffe | Paraceratherium was a dinosaur | Paraceratherium was smaller than a goat
exam: Identify the factor understood to have permitted the attainment of great size by certain insects some three hundred million years ago. | A higher concentration of oxygen within the atmosphere | The complete absence of sunlight during the Carboniferous | A diet consisting of dinosaurs | The coldness of the climate
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the extinction of the moa. | It occurred within approximately two centuries of human settlement, together with that of the Haast's eagle | It occurred sixty-six million years ago, at the same time as that of the non-bird dinosaurs, owing to the asteroid | It was caused solely by the Haast's eagle | The moa survives at the present day
exam: Ascertain the tendency observed among elephants isolated upon islands such as Sicily and Crete. | A reduction in body size to that of a pony | An increase in body size to twice the mainland form | The acquisition of the capacity for flight | The loss of the trunk
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the evidence for the existence of the creature popularly termed Bigfoot? | There is no evidence of its existence, although the extinct Gigantopithecus is known from teeth and jaws | Its existence has been conclusively demonstrated by photographs, footprints and hair examined in laboratories | It is known from a complete preserved body | It is identical to the living gorilla of China
